 ### Radius Error from Vertex
-A quality metric measuring the error committed when approximating a sphere, calculated as the distance from the sphere center to the vertices of each triangle [S3, unofficial]. It is used to compare isosurface extraction algorithms [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Definition:** Measures the deviation of triangle vertices from the ideal spherical radius [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Formula:** $\text{Total Error} = \sum_{i=1}^n \frac{R_i - R_r}{R_r}$, where $n$ is the number of triangles multiplied by 3, $R_i$ is the radius of the sphere, and $R_r$ is the distance from the center $(c_x, c_y, c_z)$ to a vertex $(v_x, v_y, v_z)$ [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Vertex Distance:** $r_i = \sqrt{(v_x - c_x)^2 + (v_y - c_y)^2 + (v_z - c_z)^2}$ [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Usage:** Lower radius error indicates a better geometric approximation of the sphere [S3, unofficial].
+A quality metric measuring the error committed when approximating a sphere, calculated as the distance from the sphere center to the vertices of each triangle [S3, unofficial]. It is used to compare isosurface extraction algorithms [S3, unofficial]. The definition measures the deviation of triangle vertices from the ideal spherical radius [S3, unofficial]. The formula is $\text{Total Error} = \sum_{i=1}^n \frac{R_i - R_r}{R_r}$, where $n$ is the number of triangles multiplied by 3, $R_i$ is the distance from the sphere center to vertex $i$ (denoted $r_i$ in the source), and $R_r$ is the reference sphere radius [S3, unofficial]. The vertex distance is calculated as $r_i = \sqrt{(v_x - c_x)^2 + (v_y - c_y)^2 + (v_z - c_z)^2}$ [S3, unofficial]. Lower radius error indicates a better geometric approximation of the sphere [S3, unofficial].
 
